{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Naphtali is a deer let loose.</b> Some say: Naphtali is honey to me — for his lips drip honey-flow from passing myrrh, by means of the good tidings he would announce to Israel. And there are those who interpret it of his fruits, which ripen quickly, and 'giving words of beauty' through the recitation of first-fruits or the blessing over fruits. And it seems right in my eyes to say that 'words of beauty' means Torah, and therefore he likened him to a deer, as is concluded in tractate Eruvin (54b): 'A beloved doe' (Proverbs 5:19) — why were words of Torah likened to a doe? To tell you: just as a doe's womb is narrow and she is beloved by her mate every hour like the first hour, so too words of Torah are beloved by those who possess them every hour like the first hour. And perhaps it was of this that they expounded 'honey to me,' for words of Torah are sweeter than honey and the honeycomb. And the meaning is: because he was called Naphtali after the fact that words of Torah are sweet as honeycomb to his mouth, therefore he will merit to be like a doe that is beloved by her mate every hour like the first hour, and he will give words of beauty — for even words of Torah that have grown old in his mouth are still words of beauty, for 'beauty' (shefer) denotes a new thing that is in its beauty and its loveliness."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:34.270142424Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}